[[:db/retract 17592186164953 :answer-field/choices 17592186164954] [:db/retract 17592186164953 :answer-field/choices 17592186164955] [:db/retract 17592186164953 :answer-field/choices 17592186164956] [:db/retract 17592186164953 :answer-field/choices 17592186164957] [:db/retract 17592186164948 :answer-field/choices 17592186164952] [:db/retract 17592186164948 :answer-field/choices 17592186164949] [:db/retract 17592186164948 :answer-field/choices 17592186164950] [:db/retract 17592186164948 :answer-field/choices 17592186164951] {:db/id 17592186166957 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-75e9cee8-821a-5456-9b71-a9061b7a8387"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166572 :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in[0,\\pi),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n![](images/9d/9d4ae844370b06d82ce892e052ecd1c114519a6bb31e153e3bcb10c2c4806d56.svg)\n\nIn our cases, for $x\\in(-\\pi,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{3}-(-x)+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166573 :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-6x-2$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x).$ \n\n![](images/4f/4f5284fb6fed9d16cafa85a546e65db1b9ebf5a5211d870b24dfe87bb1ccb49b.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-75e9cee8-821a-5456-9b71-a9061b7a8387" :question/id #uuid "75e9cee8-821a-5456-9b71-a9061b7a8387" :question/math-academy-id "q-341252" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is the value of $f(-3)?$\n\n$f(-3)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{2}{e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =(-3)^{2}{e}^{(-3)^{2}}=9{e}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-49fca85c-c10d-570a-9fbd-829efc317583"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-49fca85c-c10d-570a-9fbd-829efc317583" :answer-field/id #uuid "49fca85c-c10d-570a-9fbd-829efc317583" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-15bb385e-7f13-50d3-937e-b59ebadbf90b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15bb385e-7f13-50d3-937e-b59ebadbf90b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15bb385e-7f13-50d3-937e-b59ebadbf90b" :answer/id #uuid "15bb385e-7f13-50d3-937e-b59ebadbf90b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9e^{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186372786 :answer-field/correct "answer-5ccfdcc9-5754-55bc-8498-0746afa5815b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5ccfdcc9-5754-55bc-8498-0746afa5815b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ccfdcc9-5754-55bc-8498-0746afa5815b" :answer/id #uuid "5ccfdcc9-5754-55bc-8498-0746afa5815b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pi^{2}-2\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186314364 :answer-field/correct "answer-6291bc20-b33b-51a2-81f3-8c4774fb834c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6291bc20-b33b-51a2-81f3-8c4774fb834c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6291bc20-b33b-51a2-81f3-8c4774fb834c" :answer/id #uuid "6291bc20-b33b-51a2-81f3-8c4774fb834c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6(\\sqrt{3}-2)}{\\pi}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167288 :question/problem "Let $F(x)=2x+3$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(3+\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=2x+3$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x+3\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{2}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((2\\pi +3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{2}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{2}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{4((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(3+\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164953 :answer-field/correct "answer-e49f1d50-aff3-5106-a1bc-06b9c13d3402" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9e7d2516-8e80-5537-9f0b-2046239632d8" "answer-40d92551-833d-5ca4-a56b-7ab1b74f7b82" "answer-e49f1d50-aff3-5106-a1bc-06b9c13d3402" "answer-edfa8848-8f15-59cc-bc41-52226333544c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e7d2516-8e80-5537-9f0b-2046239632d8" :answer/id #uuid "9e7d2516-8e80-5537-9f0b-2046239632d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{\\pi n}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40d92551-833d-5ca4-a56b-7ab1b74f7b82" :answer/id #uuid "40d92551-833d-5ca4-a56b-7ab1b74f7b82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e49f1d50-aff3-5106-a1bc-06b9c13d3402" :answer/id #uuid "e49f1d50-aff3-5106-a1bc-06b9c13d3402" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edfa8848-8f15-59cc-bc41-52226333544c" :answer/id #uuid "edfa8848-8f15-59cc-bc41-52226333544c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164948 :answer-field/correct "answer-c84c6a75-c353-5602-bb33-16dd82b9c8ce" :answer-field/choices ["answer-03ab0d3f-c0ec-5341-aee5-a68517e3ea54" "answer-c84c6a75-c353-5602-bb33-16dd82b9c8ce" "answer-3ef189ae-b8a9-5aad-a7b1-aabed08f2956" "answer-ef0b3c15-c486-552b-8364-93f50ed30f9a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03ab0d3f-c0ec-5341-aee5-a68517e3ea54" :answer/id #uuid "03ab0d3f-c0ec-5341-aee5-a68517e3ea54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c84c6a75-c353-5602-bb33-16dd82b9c8ce" :answer/id #uuid "c84c6a75-c353-5602-bb33-16dd82b9c8ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ef189ae-b8a9-5aad-a7b1-aabed08f2956" :answer/id #uuid "3ef189ae-b8a9-5aad-a7b1-aabed08f2956" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef0b3c15-c486-552b-8364-93f50ed30f9a" :answer/id #uuid "ef0b3c15-c486-552b-8364-93f50ed30f9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186372778 :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-3x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{n}.$\n\n$\\,{a}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}\nfor $n\\ge 1$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168044 :tutorial/content "In the earlier topics, we saw that *symmetry* can force many Fourier coefficients to be zero automatically.\nIn particular, if a $2\\pi$-periodic function is *even*, then its Fourier series has *cosine terms only*.\n\nA **Fourier cosine series** uses this idea when a function is initially given only on the half-interval $[0,\\pi),$ but we want a full Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ (and then a $2\\pi$-periodic extension to all real $x$).\n\nTo do this, we start with a function $F(x)$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and define its **even extension** $f(x)$ to $(-\\pi,\\pi)$ by\n![](images/e2/e2e07048ac4750968947e45443f59096f395b159d4f7a846410a711194b11a14.svg)\n\nFor example, a function $F(x),$ defined on $x \\in [0,1),$ and its even extension $f(x)$ to $x \\in (-1,1)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/33/33d92f280a5672c8d9d94553ccc1ada2a9c5ea1ff12344ca8c9aeca9f44c40c6.png)\n\nBy construction, $f$ is an *even* function, meaning $f(-x)=f(x)$. Because $f$ is even, its Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ has *cosine terms only*:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos(nx)\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is even and $\\cos(nx)$ is even for any $n\\ge 1,$ the product $f(x)\\cos(nx)$ is even too. Therefore, the cosine coefficients can be computed using the *half-range formulas*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for $n\\ge 1,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo in practice, the workflow is the following:\n\n- Start with $F$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and form the even extension $f$ on $(-\\pi,\\pi).$\n\n- Compute $a_0$ and $a_n$ from the half-range integrals on $[0,\\pi).$\n\n- Write the Fourier cosine series of $f.$\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166992 :activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
